      "writtenOn": "2024-01-26T20:35:21Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yeah, the hope is to always switch to using nc.getSubscriptionIds().\n\nBut the only issue is that because telephony is not a mainline module, some other OEMs might have not populated it, so we do need a fallback. This is the reason for the wtf. Verifying that the subscriptionIds set is populated should be sufficient, but I would suggest putting it as the last in that priority:\n\nTelephonyNetworkSpecifier -\u003e WifiTransportInfo -\u003e NetworkCapabilities.SubscriptionIds\n\nThat way, we maintain where we pull the data from in all the existing cases that do work, and pull from a new source only if those fail:\n\n```\n        if (networkCapabilities.hasSingleTransportBesidesTest(TRANSPORT_CELLULAR)) {\n            subId \u003d getSubIdFromTelephonySpecifier(networkCapabilities.getNetworkSpecifier());\n        } else if (networkCapabilities.hasSingleTransportBesidesTest(TRANSPORT_WIFI)) {\n            subId \u003d getSubIdFromWifiTransportInfo(networkCapabilities.getTransportInfo());\n        } else {\n            if (mRequestRestrictedWifiEnabled \u0026\u0026 networkCapabilities.getSubscriptionIds().size() \u003d\u003d 1) {\n                subId \u003d networkCapabilities.getSubscriptionIds().toArray(new Integer[0])[0];\n            } else {\n                subId \u003d SubscriptionManager.INVALID_SUBSCRIPTION_ID;\n            }\n        }\n```\n\nAlternatively, we could check if the requestor is the carrier config app for ALL UIDs contained in the subscriptionIds set. I don\u0027t particularly see a reason why it /has/ to be limited to one, as long as all satisfy the same requirement.",
